{
  "_what": "What each actor may select, insert, update and delete in the tables the signed-in roles lost privileges on, WITH THAT PRIVILEGE GIVEN BACK (each migration's own rollback applied): i.e. what the row policies decide, the SECOND LOCK. Payroll: migration 499 took INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE from seventeen tables, 515 from leave_balances, and 549 SELECT and the rest from the twenty payroll tables the matrix observes (the tax declaration and its lines among them); the policies of migrations 262, 296, 297, 485 and 490 and the triggers of 498 decide. Bank: 550 took every privilege from bank_accounts and bank_transactions, the two of its eight tables the matrix observes; the policies of 093, 261, 370, 487, 524, 527 and 530 decide. Documents: 551 took every privilege from documents, client_documents and document_requests, the three of its five tables the matrix observes, and DROPPED the object policies of 005, 426 and 469 on storage.objects, which were the only lock on the two stored-file buckets (the rows storage/Documents and storage/year-end-exports): for those the cells are what the policies decided before 551, restored by its rollback, so the rollback is held to leave a database whose files open for the people the policies name. Settlement: 559 took INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE from the seven tables a document's settlement is derived from, receipts, receipt_allocations, purchase_payments and purchase_payment_allocations being the four of them the matrix observes (reading stays granted); the row policies on them decide. Same vocabulary as rls_expected_access.json. These are the cells that file held before each migration, unchanged; test_rls_role_by_table_matrix_pg.py::test_the_second_lock_decides_when_the_first_is_lifted fails the day a policy moves one, and the controls payroll-tier-485-reopened, payroll-tier-490-reopened and storage-second-folder-dropped are measured against it.",
